window.docContents[197].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Main-Objects-and-Hypotheses":{"contents":"An abstract weak problem is a bounded bilinear form  together with a bounded\nlinear load , and a solution satisfies\n\n\n\nThe concrete Poisson solver assumes a bounded domain in positive dimension.\nIts Dirichlet form  is built from weak gradients; Poincaré's inequality\npromotes gradient control to coercivity on . The solver is a bounded\nmap of the square-integrable load, satisfies an a priori norm estimate, and is\ncharacterized by both the variational equation and minimization of the\nDirichlet energy.\n\nVariable principal coefficients enter first as bounded operators on the gradient\ncoordinates, with pointwise  coefficient fields arriving through\nmultiplication operators. Almost-everywhere uniform ellipticity gives an\nintegrated lower bound by the Dirichlet form. Combining this bound with the\nbounded-domain Poincaré inequality gives the coercivity Lax–Milgram needs.\n\nLower-order terms can destroy positivity. What survives is a Gårding estimate,\nwhich says the form is coercive up to an  error, and adding a large enough\nmultiple of the pivot inner product turns that into a shifted coercive problem\nwith a unique weak solution and a quantitative solver bound.\n\nAlmost-everywhere uniform ellipticity and integrated uniform ellipticity are different predicates: the\nfirst describes a coefficient field almost everywhere, the second is an\nintegrated operator inequality, and passing from the first to the second is a\ntheorem.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems\u0009Weak Elliptic Problems","header":"5.4.2.2. Main Objects and Hypotheses","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Main-Objects-and-Hypotheses"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces":{"contents":"The Fourier definition is the natural whole-space model of fractional and\nnegative Sobolev regularity.  It starts from a tempered distribution ,\nmultiplies its Fourier transform by the Bessel weight\n, and asks that the result be square integrable.\nThis gives one Hilbert space  for every real order ,\nincluding negative and nonintegral orders.\n\nAt large frequencies the weight behaves like : positive order\npenalizes rapid oscillation, while negative order allows more of it. The\n in  also controls the low frequencies. For a Schwartz\nfunction, the order-one norm makes the connection with weak derivatives\nexplicit:\n\n\n\nNegative orders have an equally concrete example. The Fourier transform of\nthe point mass  is the constant function , so, for ,\nits squared  norm is finite exactly when\n\n\n\nThe integrand is bounded near zero; the tail has the same integrability as\n. Thus  exactly for\n. Negative regularity can accommodate a point source even though\nthat source has no function representative.\n\nThree representatives must be kept distinct throughout the formal theory.\n\n1. toLp u is the  representative of the Bessel potential\n.2. 𝓕 (toLp u) is the Fourier transform of toLp u; almost everywhere it\nis the weighted Fourier transform\n.3. When , toL2Fun hs u is the physical  function represented\nby the distribution  itself.\n\nThe first exists for every real order.  The third exists only at nonnegative\norder, because a negative-order Sobolev element need not be represented by a\nlocally integrable function at all.  Confusing these representatives would\nsilently erase the distinction between applying a Bessel potential to a\ndistribution and merely viewing that distribution as a function.\n\nThe Fourier transform in Mathlib uses the derivative multiplier\n.  Consequently, the symbol\n corresponds to\n, not to .  This convention\nis carried through every operator estimate and explains why the Fourier norm\nand the project's integer graph norm are equivalent but, away from order\nzero, not equal.\n\nSeveral Sobolev scales are in use at once. The Zulip thread\nFrequency-side Sobolev definitions\nrecords overlapping definitions being developed at the same time:\n through the Fourier multiplier,  through weak derivatives, and\nthe Sobolev–Slobodeckij and Besov scales. The comparisons proved here are\n\nfor natural , and the equivalence of the completed whole-space Gagliardo\nspace with real  for , both with explicit norm bounds (for the\nsecond, when ). Mathlib's general Bessel-potential definition uses a\nphysical  representative; its weighted Fourier characterization is\nproved at . We keep these definitions distinct and state comparisons\nwith their precise orders and exponents.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Fourier Sobolev Spaces","header":"4.5.1. Fourier and Fractional Sobolev Spaces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces"}});